{"subjectId":"c","id":"command-line-args","title":"Command-Line Arguments","group":"Files, Preprocessor & Program Organisation","order":48,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>main(int argc, char *argv[])</code> receives the words typed after the program name: <code>argc</code> counts them, <code>argv</code> holds them as strings, and <code>argv[argc]</code> is always <code>NULL</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — run as: ./prog input.txt --verbose 42</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n    printf(\"argc = %d\\n\", argc);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; argc; i++)\n        printf(\"argv[%d] = \\\"%s\\\"\\n\", i, argv[i]);\n    printf(\"argv[argc] is %s\\n\", argv[argc] == NULL ? \"NULL\" : \"not NULL\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>argc = 4\nargv[0] = \"./prog\"\nargv[1] = \"input.txt\"\nargv[2] = \"--verbose\"\nargv[3] = \"42\"\nargv[argc] is NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checks\"></i>Converting and validating</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — run as: ./prog 10 20 x7 -5</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n#include &lt;errno.h&gt;\n\nint parse_int(const char *s, long *out) {\n    char *end;\n    errno = 0;\n    long v = strtol(s, &amp;end, 10);\n    if (end == s || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) return -1;\n    *out = v;\n    return 0;\n}\n\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n    if (argc &lt; 2) {\n        fprintf(stderr, \"usage: %s NUM...\\n\", argv[0]);\n        return EXIT_FAILURE;\n    }\n    long sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt; argc; i++) {\n        long v;\n        if (parse_int(argv[i], &amp;v) != 0) { printf(\"skipping '%s'\\n\", argv[i]); continue; }\n        sum += v;\n    }\n    printf(\"sum = %ld\\n\", sum);\n    return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>skipping 'x7'\nsum = 25</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments-horizontal\"></i>Parsing options</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — run as: ./prog -n 2 -v C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n    int verbose = 0, count = 1;\n    const char *name = \"world\";\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt; argc; i++) {\n        if (strcmp(argv[i], \"-v\") == 0) verbose = 1;\n        else if (strcmp(argv[i], \"-n\") == 0 &amp;&amp; i + 1 &lt; argc) count = atoi(argv[++i]);\n        else name = argv[i];\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; count; i++) printf(\"hello, %s\\n\", name);\n    if (verbose) printf(\"(printed %d times)\\n\", count);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hello, C\nhello, C\n(printed 2 times)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>On POSIX systems, <code>getopt()</code> (<code>&lt;unistd.h&gt;</code>) and GNU <code>getopt_long()</code> handle combined flags (<code>-vn 2</code>), <code>--</code> and error messages for you.</p></div>\n<ul><li><code>argv[0]</code> is the program name <em>as invoked</em> — it may be a path, or anything the parent process chose.</li><li>The shell does quoting and globbing: <code>./prog \"a b\" *.c</code> passes <code>a b</code> as one argument and expands <code>*.c</code> before your program starts.</li><li>The strings in <code>argv</code> are modifiable and live until the program ends.</li><li>Environment variables: <code>getenv(\"HOME\")</code> from <code>&lt;stdlib.h&gt;</code>.</li></ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is argv[argc]?</summary><div><p>Always a null pointer — the standard guarantees it. You can loop with <code>for (char **p = argv; *p; p++)</code> instead of counting.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is argv declared as char *argv[] and char **argv interchangeably?</summary><div><p>Because an array parameter is adjusted to a pointer: <code>char *argv[]</code> in a parameter list <em>is</em> <code>char **argv</code>. It points to the first element of an array of <code>char *</code>, each pointing to a NUL-terminated argument string.</p></div></details></section>"}